  "scholarly-hir.title": {
    "text": "Healthy Aging Individual Project——HIR prize winning report",
    "src": "docx-2026-10-brief.md#段[7]",
    "lang": "en"
  },
  "scholarly-hir.no": {
    "text": "01.4",
    "chrome": true,
    "lang": "en"
  },
  "scholarly-hir.answerLabel": {
    "text": "Answer:",
    "src": "content-brief.md#段[72]",
    "lang": "en"
  },
  "scholarly-hir.q1.no": {
    "text": "1",
    "src": "content-brief.md#段[71]",
    "lang": "en"
  },
  "scholarly-hir.q1": {
    "text": "What does “healthy aging” actually mean?",
    "src": "content-brief.md#段[71]",
    "lang": "en"
  },
  "scholarly-hir.a1": {
    "text": "It sounds simple, but it has no clear definition. In medicine, a disease has a defined population, a measurable endpoint, and a clear outcome. “Healthy aging” has none of these. It is multi-system, gradual, and changes over time—so it is hard to define, evaluate, or commercialize by regular standards.",
    "src": "content-brief.md#段[73]",
    "lang": "en"
  },
  "scholarly-hir.bridge1": {
    "text": "So if we can’t even define it, how do we prove a treatment works? That’s exactly where the real problem begins.",
    "src": "content-brief.md#段[75]",
    "lang": "en"
  },
  "scholarly-hir.q2.no": {
    "text": "2",
    "src": "content-brief.md#段[75]",
    "lang": "en"
  },
  "scholarly-hir.q2": {
    "text": "Is current medical technology helping us live longer, or live better?",
    "src": "content-brief.md#段[75]",
    "lang": "en"
  },
  "scholarly-hir.a2": {
    "text": "Mostly longer. Over the past century, human lifespan has been extended, but advanced biomedical innovation still focuses on discrete diseases—one disease, one treatment. The multi-system decline that actually determines whether older people can maintain functional capacity is often treated as less important, because it doesn’t fit the usual standards.",
    "src": "content-brief.md#段[77]",
    "lang": "en"
  },
  "scholarly-hir.bridge2": {
    "text": "But what if the thing that matters most is exactly the thing our system is worst at measuring?",
    "src": "content-brief.md#段[79]",
    "lang": "en"
  },
  "scholarly-hir.q3.no": {
    "text": "3",
    "src": "content-brief.md#段[81]",
    "lang": "en"
  },
  "scholarly-hir.q3": {
    "text": "Aging isn’t a disease—so how do you treat it?",
    "src": "content-brief.md#段[81]",
    "lang": "en"
  },
  "scholarly-hir.a3": {
    "text": "That’s the core dilemma. Age-related decline usually shows up as multiple diseases at once. The body loses its ability to clear senescent cells efficiently, and their accumulation damages surrounding tissues. Any treatment must clear the damaging ones while preserving the short-lived senescent cells that support repair. Synthetic biology offers a way to build circuits that respond to the body’s own signals—automatic, precise, and adaptive. But without changing where investment goes, the technology may still end up serving the same old direction.",
    "src": "content-brief.md#段[83]",
    "lang": "en"
  },
  "scholarly-hir.closing": {
    "text": "So, the science is ready. The question is whether the system is.",
    "src": "content-brief.md#段[85]",
    "lang": "en"
  },
